-- CONTROL — the SHARP one. It shares the method name and MUST still be invariant.
--
-- The other control (control-distinct-method-names-invariant/) flips the method name,
-- so a reader can dismiss it as "of course two unrelated interfaces don't interact".
-- This one does NOT: `E1` and `E2` both declare a method named `n`, exactly as the
-- ledgered cases do. The single dimension that differs is that their impls are on
-- DISJOINT receiver types (`U` and `V`), so at each call site only one impl can match.
--
-- ⭐ WHY THAT MATTERS FOR WHAT THIS GATE CLAIMS. Every ledgered row here is evidence
-- for a specific proposition -- that the candidate set is keyed by BARE METHOD NAME
-- and therefore deformed by interface-declaration order. If THIS case also diverged,
-- that proposition would be too weak: the finding would be "sharing a method name at
-- all is order-sensitive", regardless of whether the receivers can collide. Keeping
-- it green is what makes the ledgered rows evidence about the CANDIDATE SET rather
-- than about method names in general.
--
-- Unledgered on purpose, so the gate grades it as invariant and reds if it moves.
interface E1 a where
  n : a -> Int

interface E2 a where
  n : a -> Int

data U = U

data V = V

impl E1 U where
  n _ = 1

impl E2 V where
  n _ = 2

main = println (n U + n V)
